You are helping build "tallybook", a stdlib-only Python (3.9+) double-entry bookkeeping tool in /tmp/hzt-3388928427/bench-run-zs8udc0m/clone. The full spec is brief.md there; it is exact and hidden tests will check the HTTP API closely (status codes, headers, JSON bodies). The whole library is already implemented and tested under tallybook/ (money, currency, dates, accounts, model, ledger, journal, query, rules, csvimport, budgets, recurring, export, reports, storage). Read brief.md's "Conventions" section and the whole "## The web API" section word by word, plus the storage section; skim the public APIs of tallybook/storage.py (Store), export.py (transaction_to_dict, money_to_dict, to_json), reports.py, budgets.py, recurring.py.

Your job: implement the package **tallybook/api/** (an empty __init__.py exists), with `from tallybook.api.server import serve`, `serve(db_path, host="127.0.0.1", port=0, today=None)`:
- opens the book once first (Store.open(db_path)) and checks it is at the latest version (store.version == LATEST_VERSION, else raise the storage ConflictError ``database is at version N, run `tallybook db migrate` ``), raising storage errors before printing anything;
- uses http.server ThreadingHTTPServer; prints exactly `tallybook listening on http://HOST:PORT` (real port) flushed to stdout, nothing else ever (override log_message to be silent; no tracebacks to stderr — override handle_error too);
- serves until SIGINT or SIGTERM (install signal handlers when on the main thread; make shutdown clean), closes the socket, returns 0;
- each request opens its own Store, commits before answering; Content-Length on every answer and `Connection: close`;
- implements every route, verb, the 404/405 (with Allow header, sorted, joined by ", ")/413/415/400 ordering rules, the query-string and body rules, money input in both forms, the error body {"error": msg}, status mapping by the library error's .code (parse/validation→400, not_found→404, conflict→409), HEAD/OPTIONS→405 etc. The 413 case must still deliver the answer even though the client sends the whole body (read and discard the body, or otherwise make sure the client gets the response without a connection reset). A server-side unexpected exception should still answer (500 with an error body) and print nothing.
- A separate agent is writing the CLI in parallel; the CLI's `serve` command will call your serve() with db_path (a str), host, port and today (a datetime.date or None). Do not edit tallybook/cli/.

Do NOT edit files outside tallybook/api/ and your own tests/test_api.py. If you find a library bug, describe it precisely in your final report (and work around it in the API only if harmless).

Test thoroughly with unittest (tests/test_api.py): start serve() in a background thread or a subprocess on port 0, parse the printed port, and exercise every endpoint with http.client, including error cases (unknown path, 405 + Allow, 413 at 1048577 bytes but OK at exactly 1048576, 415, bad JSON, unknown field, percent-encoded account paths, `?q=` queries, imports both ways, reports in json and text, export formats, recurring materialize twice). Make sure tests clean up their servers. Final report: summary, ambiguities you resolved, library issues found. Do not commit.

